WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.184
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.184 --> 00:00:08.150
Think different.

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:12.471
We've all seen these Apple commercials.

00:00:12.471 --> 00:00:16.270
Get creative, innovate.

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:21.500
Our society encourages new ideas and
perspectives, yet

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:27.040
often stigmatizes people who
neurologically think differently.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.996
How many of you have or
know someone with dyslexia?

00:00:31.996 --> 00:00:34.609
How about ADHD?

00:00:34.609 --> 00:00:39.401
What about someone on the autism spectrum?

00:00:39.401 --> 00:00:40.572
Yeah, we all do.

00:00:40.572 --> 00:00:43.795
One in 58 people have autism.

00:00:43.795 --> 00:00:50.160
One in ten, ADHD,
another one in ten have dyslexia.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:57.020
And we have always been
neurologically different yet

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:03.140
our education systems,
our schools, our social norms,

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:08.510
are all designed for
the majority or neurotypical mind.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:15.430
We seek to think differently, yet
we exclude different thinkers.

00:01:18.519 --> 00:01:20.261
This is my little brother, Steven.

00:01:22.443 --> 00:01:22.977
He's 24.

00:01:22.977 --> 00:01:28.746
He is a painter, a philosopher,
a mental health advocate,

00:01:28.746 --> 00:01:32.260
and also an awesome little brother.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:34.652
He has autism.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:43.129
For those of you who aren't familiar,
the autism spectrum is a continuum

00:01:43.129 --> 00:01:48.810
of traits that impacts how a person
perceives or socializes with others.

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:55.570
This difference in brain development
can cause mild to severe impairments.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.410
It is a wide spectrum.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:02.572
And it affects no two people the same way.

00:02:02.572 --> 00:02:07.170
For Steven, he is able to communicate.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:12.010
And he understands that he has autism,
that he thinks differently.

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:17.643
And yet sometimes, his autism can make it
really difficult to connect with others.

00:02:22.081 --> 00:02:26.957
But I am excited for
this time period in history, diversity and

00:02:26.957 --> 00:02:30.920
inclusion are being sought after and
celebrated.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:35.370
Brave change makers have fought
the injustices of authoring,

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:39.209
and we're finally seeing and
appreciating the value of our differences.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:47.350
There is a long way to go across
previous divides around race,

00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:49.380
gender, and sexual orientation.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:54.386
Yet I don't see inclusion as zero of some,
and

00:02:54.386 --> 00:03:00.410
I'd love to add to this rich conversation,
the concept of neurodiversity.

00:03:04.073 --> 00:03:08.955
Neurodiversity is a term first
coined in 1988 by Judy Singer,

00:03:08.955 --> 00:03:11.900
the Australian sociologist.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:15.600
And it is a concept that regards
individuals with differences

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:16.940
in brain function and

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:21.898
behavioral traits, as part of the normal
variation in the human population.

00:03:21.898 --> 00:03:26.470
Because like biodiversity
that accounts for

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:31.970
the rich vibrance in our natural
environment, neurodiversity accounts for

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:35.520
the fact that all of our brains
are neurologically distinct.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:40.078
Whether it is autism, ADHD or
another neurodivergency,

00:03:40.078 --> 00:03:42.968
we really all do think differently.

00:03:45.565 --> 00:03:52.728
I fell in love with this term Because
it doesn't focus first on impairment.

00:04:03.065 --> 00:04:07.234
My hope is that through
the lens of neurodiversity,

00:04:07.234 --> 00:04:12.636
we can celebrate each others
differences and see the person first,

00:04:12.636 --> 00:04:16.350
and not the disability or
different ability.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.960
My hope is to increase your awareness,
your understanding, and

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:29.350
encourage inclusion to build
neurodiverse communities.

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:32.820
Because you can't have inclusion
without increasing your understanding,

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:36.800
and this understanding
requires a broader awareness.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.630
Without these three elements, if you
interact with a neurologically different

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:46.300
person, you may be thinking to
yourself things like, what a weirdo!

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:47.840
What a pity?

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:50.450
This isn't my problem.

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:52.980
These thoughts,

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:57.160
I'd love to change instead into a
conversation where we build relationship.

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:02.300
I'd like to take you through my own
journey and all three of these steps.

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:05.950
Let's start with awareness.

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:12.510
As I shared,
I grew up in a neurodiverse home, through

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:16.720
conversations with my brother, I learned
a lot around how autism affects him.

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:22.660
And also through seeing how other
people treat him when we're in public.

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:26.870
If Steven is feeling
overwhelmed in his environment,

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:31.812
because of the sensory changes
with autism, to cope or soothe,

00:05:31.812 --> 00:05:38.238
he'll pace back and forth, he may swing
his arms or scratch his head repeatively.

00:05:40.457 --> 00:05:43.807
And I've watched how others
respond to this behavior.

00:05:43.807 --> 00:05:48.613
I've seen even neighbors move away.

00:05:51.185 --> 00:05:53.445
Maybe to the other side of the street,

00:05:53.445 --> 00:05:56.140
even shield their
children from my brother.

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:03.576
And I wonder if they think what bullies
have called him his whole life or

00:06:03.576 --> 00:06:04.616
said to him.

00:06:10.224 --> 00:06:11.115
What a weirdo!

00:06:11.115 --> 00:06:15.680
Yet I believe all these
people to be good people.

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:21.030
And that if they understood he was
different or how he was different,

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:25.252
if they were aware of the differences
of neurodiversity and autism,

00:06:25.252 --> 00:06:28.191
then instead they would
start a conversation.

00:06:30.932 --> 00:06:34.703
They would say, hello, how are you?

00:06:39.014 --> 00:06:40.549
The next step is understanding.

00:06:43.208 --> 00:06:47.169
I spent my summer living in
intentionally inclusive community,

00:06:47.169 --> 00:06:51.570
where neurodiverse people lived
together and shared housing and life.

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:57.503
And I found even myself thinking this,
what a pity?

00:06:57.503 --> 00:07:02.170
What a pity that my new
roommates have challenges around

00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:05.655
things that I take for granted every day?

00:07:05.655 --> 00:07:09.624
And when it comes to different abilities,

00:07:09.624 --> 00:07:15.586
this can be the default, but
it doesn't have to be our default.

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:22.794
My understanding increased as I built real
relationships with people different from

00:07:22.794 --> 00:07:23.306
myself.

00:07:23.306 --> 00:07:30.240
I met Nadia and
we went dancing all night long.

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:35.470
And Marie, whose love of music took us
to the local music school for lessons.

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:40.043
And my neurodivergent friend Carry,
who despite being only able to communicate

00:07:40.043 --> 00:07:44.495
through eye blinks, got her best friend
laughing up a storm every single day.

00:07:47.002 --> 00:07:53.870
I learned through my understanding that I
was wrong to start from a place of pity.

00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:59.400
And instead, I want to start from a place
of curiosity, expecting connection,

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:01.150
relatability, and friendship.

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:05.540
I started saying, who are you?

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:06.040
Instead.

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:11.470
The next step is inclusion.

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:13.860
I'd like you to meet Cole.

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:19.720
Cole is the president of spectrum
autism at the University of Berkeley.

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:23.870
And when he joined the autism club,

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:27.045
he found himself to be the only
autistic person in it.

00:08:27.045 --> 00:08:30.140
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Think for

00:08:30.140 --> 00:08:34.965
a moment if our women in management
group here were led by a man.

00:08:34.965 --> 00:08:38.938
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Or if there were no women in it.

00:08:38.938 --> 00:08:41.150
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:47.220
Cole shared with me his story and
his journey to college and

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:51.790
how at times he would hide his
autism to avoid stigma, and

00:08:51.790 --> 00:08:55.820
how difficult this made
building genuine community.

00:08:55.820 --> 00:09:01.560
So when he arrived at Cal and met students
interested in building neurodiverse

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:07.810
communities, he was embraced and now he's
leading the charge for inclusion there.

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:12.589
He says, I am the first president
of spectrum autism at Cal, but

00:09:12.589 --> 00:09:14.282
I will not be the last.

00:09:16.255 --> 00:09:19.997
With all the challenges we
have in the world today,

00:09:19.997 --> 00:09:24.608
it's not uncommon to think this,
that this is not my problem,

00:09:24.608 --> 00:09:28.101
this is not my issue,
this is not my community.

00:09:29.473 --> 00:09:35.567
Whether it is in our schools, in our work,
certainly in our neighborhoods,

00:09:35.567 --> 00:09:39.340
there are neurodivergent
people everywhere.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:45.708
And oftentimes, especially with
the challenges of connecting with autism,

00:09:45.708 --> 00:09:47.500
there's a lot of isolation.

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:56.670
How can we take one step closer and
make it a little easier to connect?

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:02.406
How can we change this is not my problem,
to this is my opportunity, and

00:10:02.406 --> 00:10:04.003
we are in this together.

00:10:06.834 --> 00:10:12.434
Hopefully, I have increased your
awareness, increased your understanding,

00:10:12.434 --> 00:10:17.140
and encouraged you to think a little
differently about inclusion.

00:10:18.770 --> 00:10:19.323
How do you start?

00:10:22.794 --> 00:10:25.380
To say hello, how are you?

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:27.550
Who are you?

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:32.428
We are in this together.

00:10:32.428 --> 00:10:36.624
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:36.624 --> 00:10:39.732
[MUSIC]

